  "assumptions": [
    "All turnaround phase durations and 30-day / 180-day service-active intervals are synthetic what-if inputs, not measured Sceye or other operator maintenance data.",
    "The Sceye ST1 30-day result is a US-Japan round-trip mission including transit and about a week of operations over Japan; it is not treated as a measured 30-day on-station interval.",
    "Payload reported Sceye's then-current recovered-platform teardown/testing/reuse turnaround as measured in months, with an ambition to reduce it to weeks; no exact turnaround-day value was reported. The 14/30/60/90/180-day values are sensitivity scenarios only.",
    "The six-month service-active interval is a stretch scenario based on a reported platform endurance ambition, not demonstrated endurance.",
    "Turnaround components are assumed to be sequential and non-overlapping: recovery/decommission, transport, inspection/disassembly, repair/refurbishment, replenishment, launch preparation and transit to service area.",
    "All aircraft are identical, each supplies one unit of service capacity while on station, and service demand is three units continuously.",
    "All aircraft rotations are assumed to be perfectly phase-staggered and fully schedulable, with unlimited launch/recovery capacity, maintenance bays, ground support and spares.",
    "The lower-bound formula is ceil(required_service_units / service_duty_fraction), where service_duty_fraction = service_active_days / (service_active_days + turnaround_days).",
    "This formula is an aggregate long-run capacity lower bound only; it does not prove zero outage, an exact schedule, weather resilience, failure tolerance, launch-window feasibility, repair distributions or fleet availability SLA.",
    "No common-cause weather, independent failure, spares failure, correlated component lot, gateway/backhaul, spectrum, cyber or maintenance-resource queueing is modelled.",
    "The scenario results are not an apples-to-apples fleet count for current commercial HAPS operators."
  ]
}
